<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml">

    <!--
        ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        THE GEOMETRY-AND-STATES FLOOR

        This file is not decoration and it is not redundant. Do not delete it because
        "the keyed Dm* styles already cover everything" — they do not, and the way we
        found out is recorded below.

        WHAT IT IS

        A floor of TargetType-only styles for Button, Entry, Editor and Picker, plus the
        Label family default and the Page / Shell chrome. It supplies the properties a
        control needs when nobody has said otherwise: minimum touch targets, padding,
        corner radius, and Button interaction states.

        THE INVARIANT: NO COLOUR, NO FONT, AT CONTROL LEVEL

        The Button / Entry / Editor / Picker styles set no TextColor, BackgroundColor,
        PlaceholderColor, FontFamily or FontSize. Those belong exclusively to the keyed
        Dm* styles in Controls.Base.xaml and its derivatives; putting them here too would
        give the app two sources of truth for the same property. The Label style is the
        single, deliberate exception — one FontFamily setter, explained where it sits.

        Page and Shell do carry colour tokens. They are app chrome with exactly one
        instance each and no keyed alternative to defer to, so the invariant is about the
        control-level styles, not about this file as a whole.

        WHY IT EXISTS — measured, not assumed

        Task 10 deleted the stock MAUI template's Resources/Styles/Styles.xaml. Those
        stock implicit styles were quietly load-bearing, and removing them without a
        replacement was a regression twice over:

          1. GEOMETRY. 16 of the app's 20 Button elements carry no Style= at all, and all
             3 Entry elements are likewise unstyled or only partly styled. They took
             Padding, CornerRadius and the 44px minimum touch target from the stock
             styles. MAUI leaves MinimumHeightRequest unset by default, so without this
             floor they simply shrink. This was measured, not predicted: a pixel diff of
             the running app against the pre-deletion build showed the DmSearchBar Entry
             on the dungeon list page dropping from 51px tall to 42px, with everything
             below it shifting up 9px — and dropping the field under the 44px touch
             target the Button style is careful to keep.

          2. INTERACTION STATES. The stock Button style also carried a CommonStates group
             (hover and press opacity). Task 4b measured only padding and min-size and
             missed it, so the first cut of this file left every unstyled Button with no
             hover or press feedback at all. See the Button style below.

        Editor and Picker get the same minimums even though, today, both are inert: every
        Editor in the app sets an explicit height, and ObjectPaletteView.xaml.cs sets
        cb.MinWidth = 0 on the Windows ComboBox behind the one Picker. They are here for
        consistency — so the next Editor or Picker added to the app gets the same floor
        every Button and Entry already has, rather than silently getting none.

        THERE IS STILL NO IMPLICIT Border STYLE — and that one is a measured conclusion

        The stock template had one (Stroke Gray200/Gray500, StrokeShape Rectangle,
        StrokeThickness 1). It is the one case where "deliberately thin" survived contact
        with measurement. The Task 3 audit walked all 33 real Border elements — the
        often-quoted 61 counted 28 property elements such as the Shadow and StrokeShape
        sub-elements as if they were Borders — and found that NONE of them paints the
        stock grey: 21 set Stroke themselves, 5 receive it from a keyed style or from
        visual states, and 7 set StrokeThickness to zero. Nothing was lost by dropping it,
        so every Border states its own intent at the point of use rather than inheriting
        an outline nobody chose.

        Exactly one Border property was genuinely inherited: AreaSidebarItem.xaml set no
        StrokeShape and took Rectangle from the stock style. It is re-declared explicitly
        at AreaSidebarItem.xaml:15.

        THE BoxView QUESTION, ASKED AND ANSWERED: NO LOSS

        The stock BoxView style set BackgroundColor to Gray950/Gray200. Three BoxViews set
        a semi-transparent Color and no background of their own, which on paper means that
        grey composited behind them and no longer does:

            NotebookView.xaml:136      Color DmHairlineStrong  (alpha 42%)
            NotebookView.xaml:268      Color DmHairline        (alpha 28%)
            FloorSidebarItem.xaml:101  Color DmHairline        (alpha 28%)

        Measured rather than argued: all three were photographed on both sides of the
        deletion (the notebook with its header and footer rules open, and a floor expanded
        so FloorSidebarItem's AreaSeparator is visible — it is IsVisible="False" until
        then). All came out at ZERO differing pixels.

        WHY, verified — because a wrong mechanism here is worse than none, and the first
        draft of this note had one. It claimed BoxView "paints Color over its full bounds
        so the background never reached a pixel", which cannot be right: a 28%-alpha fill
        composites with whatever is beneath it rather than erasing it, and that is the very
        premise that predicted a loss.

        The actual reason is that BackgroundColor never reaches the platform view at all.
        Two runtime probes on AreaSeparator, on Windows:

          1. Setting BackgroundColor="Red" on it renders nothing red. The bar draws only
             its semi-transparent DmHairline fill over the sidebar surface.
          2. AreaSeparator.Handler.PlatformView is a
             Microsoft.Maui.Graphics.Win2D.W2DGraphicsView — a drawing canvas, not a
             Control, Border or Panel. MAUI's Windows background mapper has no target on
             it. The MAUI-side property is set correctly (the probe read back
             Alpha=1 Red=1); it simply has nowhere to go.

        So the stock style's BackgroundColor was inert on every BoxView in the app, opaque
        Color or not. There is deliberately no implicit BoxView style and nothing was lost.

        For the record, had the loss been real the fix would NOT have been to restore the
        grey — that would put stock template colour back into the theme layer, which is
        the exact thing this refactor removes.

        THE Label DEFAULT

        Exists only so no text falls back to a platform font now that the OpenSans
        registrations have been removed (Task 10).

        Three Labels genuinely still depend on it, and they are the only text in the app
        whose font actually changed when the stock styles went. All three are built in
        code-behind, which is why the Task 3 audit — an XAML sweep — never listed them and
        Tasks 5-8 never gave them an explicit family:

            ObjectPaletteView.xaml.cs:282     the "···" category expander glyph
            ObjectPaletteView.xaml.cs:262     single-letter fallback for an object with no image
            SecondaryPaletteView.xaml.cs:50   the same fallback in the secondary palette

        They rendered in OpenSansRegular by accident and now render in EB Garamond by
        design, which is the intended destination. The measured effect on the one that is
        routinely on screen (the "···") is 84 differing pixels: the glyph sits about 2px
        lower. Left as-is deliberately — inheriting DmFontBody from this style IS the
        correct answer for them; they are named here so the change is on the record rather
        than discovered later as a mystery.

        THE Shell STYLE

        Keeps the four TabBar setters carried by the stock style it replaces. AppShell
        currently declares a single ShellContent and shows no tab bar, so they paint
        nothing today; they are retained so that this dictionary is a strict superset of
        what it supersedes and adding a second Shell item cannot regress the chrome.
        ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->

    <Style TargetType="Label">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontBody}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        Geometry-only implicit Button style.

        This exists solely so that deleting the stock Button style in the now-removed
        Resources/Styles/Styles.xaml (Task 10) was a geometry no-op. The Task 4b
        measurement walked every Button in the app and found 18 of the 21 then present
        relied on
        that stock style for at least one of Padding, MinimumHeightRequest or
        CornerRadius; MAUI leaves MinimumHeightRequest unset by default, so
        removing the stock style without this one would shrink buttons below the
        44px minimum touch target across the whole app.

        Colour and font are deliberately excluded here. They remain the exclusive
        property of the keyed DmButton* styles in Controls.Base.xaml and its
        derivatives; duplicating either here would give the app two sources of
        truth for the same property.

        THE RADIUS IS NOW A TOKEN, AND IT MOVED. An earlier revision of this comment
        said the corner radius "mirrors the stock Styles.xaml value it replaces and is
        not a candidate for a new radius token". That was true only while the theme
        migration held pixel-identity. That constraint is lifted, and an 8 here was
        never a design decision — it is the .NET MAUI template's own default, inherited
        verbatim from a stock style this project has since deleted. It made every
        unstyled Button in the app twice as round as every other rounded surface, all
        of which sit on 2, 3 or 4.

        It is now bound to DmRadiusLoose (4). This is the most visible single change in
        the design-system-completion sweep: every Button that carries no Style= — 16 of
        the app's 20 — changes shape. That is intended. Buttons now match the cards,
        popups, chips and panels around them.

        The remaining four values stay literals, and each for its own reason rather
        than for want of a token:

          Padding 14,10          matches DmPaddingPanel by value, but a Button is not a
                                 panel. Binding a popup-body role here would make a
                                 later rescale of that role silently resize every
                                 button in the app. The button footprint is settled by
                                 adopting the paired DmButton* styles (spec §4.4), not
                                 by borrowing a role that happens to share its numbers.
          MinimumHeightRequest   44, and MinimumWidthRequest 44 — the platform touch
          MinimumWidthRequest    target, not a spacing step.
          BorderWidth 0          zero means "none"; it is the absence of a stroke
                                 rather than the smallest one on the stroke ladder.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,10" />
        <Setter Property="MinimumHeightRequest" Value="44" />
        <Setter Property="MinimumWidthRequest" Value="44" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource DmRadiusLoose}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderWidth" Value="0" />

        <!--
            Interaction states. Opacity is not colour, so this does not breach the rule
            above: the style still sets no TextColor, BackgroundColor, FontFamily or
            FontSize. 16 of the app's 20 Buttons carry no Style= and therefore relied on
            the stock implicit style for hover and press feedback; without this group they
            would have none at all.

            Normal, PointerOver and Pressed are byte-identical to the stock values (1,
            0.82, 0.7), so hover and press are unchanged.

            THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO Disabled STATE HERE, and the reason is a precedence
            rule that is easy to miss: a VisualState setter outranks BOTH a DataTrigger and
            a local value set on the element. Anything this group put on Disabled would
            silently overrule what a call site had chosen for itself.

            Three unstyled Buttons do choose for themselves, all at Opacity 0.35:

                DungeonDetailPage StartSessionButton  DataTrigger FloorCount == 0
                DungeonListPage   StartSessionButton  Start Session icon, same trigger
                DungeonDetailPage UndoButton          local IsEnabled + Opacity

            Named rather than cited by line: the line numbers here were wrong on all three
            within one wave of being written, which is the whole reason the census above was
            recounted. A name survives an edit above it.

            An earlier draft of this file set Disabled to Opacity 0.45 to match
            DmButtonBase. That was wrong twice over: it overrode all three of those sites,
            rendering them LESS dim than their authors chose and weakening the disabled
            affordance — and it did not match stock either. The stock Disabled state set
            TextColor and BackgroundColor to Gray950/Gray200/Gray600 and NO Opacity at all.

            Omitting the state therefore reproduces stock's opacity behaviour exactly for
            those three sites and merely drops the greys, which was the goal: those came
            from the template palette deleted with Resources/Styles/Colors.xaml in Task 10,
            and reinstating them would drag stock template colour back into the theme
            layer. A Button that wants a disabled look states it at the point of use, or
            opts into a keyed DmButton* style, which carries its own Disabled state.
        -->
        <Setter Property="VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups">
            <VisualStateGroupList>
                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.82" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.7" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                </VisualStateGroup>
            </VisualStateGroupList>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        Geometry-only implicit Entry, Editor and Picker styles.

        The two setters on each are copied verbatim from the stock styles they replace
        (d7caa72:Resources/Styles/Styles.xaml — Editor :111-112, Entry :133-134,
        Picker :196-197), which set MinimumHeightRequest and MinimumWidthRequest to 44 on
        all three. Nothing else from those stock styles is carried: TextColor,
        BackgroundColor, PlaceholderColor, FontFamily, FontSize and the Disabled visual
        state are all deliberately omitted per the invariant at the top of this file, and
        every Entry, Editor and Picker in the app already sets its own colour and font.

        Scope, stated precisely because it is easy to get wrong: a MAUI implicit style is
        ignored outright by any element that carries Style=, so this floor reaches exactly
        the unstyled Entry / Editor / Picker elements — the same set the stock style
        reached, and no more. The keyed DmEntryBase / DmEditorBase / DmPickerBase styles
        set no minimums, so a control opting into one opts out of the floor, precisely as
        it did before Task 10. That is preserved behaviour, not a gap: CreateDungeonPopup's
        Entry and Editor never had the 44px minimum in the first place.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="Entry">
        <Setter Property="MinimumHeightRequest" Value="44" />
        <Setter Property="MinimumWidthRequest" Value="44" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="Editor">
        <Setter Property="MinimumHeightRequest" Value="44" />
        <Setter Property="MinimumWidthRequest" Value="44" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="Picker">
        <Setter Property="MinimumHeightRequest" Value="44" />
        <Setter Property="MinimumWidthRequest" Value="44" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="Page" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{StaticResource DmSurfaceTavern}" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="Shell" ApplyToDerivedTypes="True">
        <Setter Property="Shell.BackgroundColor" Value="{StaticResource DmSurfaceTavern}" />
        <Setter Property="Shell.ForegroundColor" Value="{StaticResource DmAccentGold}" />
        <Setter Property="Shell.TitleColor" Value="{StaticResource DmTextTavern}" />
        <Setter Property="Shell.DisabledColor" Value="{StaticResource DmTextTavernMuted}" />
        <Setter Property="Shell.UnselectedColor" Value="{StaticResource DmTextTavernMuted}" />
        <Setter Property="Shell.NavBarHasShadow" Value="False" />
        <Setter Property="Shell.TabBarBackgroundColor" Value="{StaticResource DmSurfaceTavern}" />
        <Setter Property="Shell.TabBarForegroundColor" Value="{StaticResource DmAccentGold}" />
        <Setter Property="Shell.TabBarTitleColor" Value="{StaticResource DmAccentGold}" />
        <Setter Property="Shell.TabBarUnselectedColor" Value="{StaticResource DmTextTavernMuted}" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
